1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,124
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,304 --> 00:00:04,287
This is the video I've been building

3
00:00:04,287 --> 00:00:04,673
toward.

4
00:00:04,853 --> 00:00:06,884
Everything this channel teaches,

5
00:00:06,884 --> 00:00:07,709
in one place.

6
00:00:07,889 --> 00:00:10,169
The complete playbook from broke to one

7
00:00:10,169 --> 00:00:11,105
million dollars.

8
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:12,300
Not through luck,

9
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:13,733
not through a hot stock,

10
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:15,882
and not through a six figure salary.

11
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:18,883
Through nine stages that ordinary people can

12
00:00:18,883 --> 00:00:20,613
follow, one step at a time.

13
00:00:20,963 --> 00:00:23,274
Let's be clear about what broke means.

14
00:00:23,454 --> 00:00:25,451
Maybe you have zero in savings.

15
00:00:25,631 --> 00:00:27,892
Maybe you have credit card debt and

16
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:28,603
a car loan.

17
00:00:28,783 --> 00:00:30,884
Maybe your net worth is negative.

18
00:00:31,064 --> 00:00:32,597
That's where many people start.

19
00:00:32,777 --> 00:00:35,587
In fact, according to the Federal Reserve's

20
00:00:35,587 --> 00:00:37,417
Survey of Consumer Finances,

21
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:40,293
a meaningful share of American families have

22
00:00:40,293 --> 00:00:41,535
negative net worth,

23
00:00:41,535 --> 00:00:43,888
meaning they owe more than they own.

24
00:00:44,238 --> 00:00:45,747
And one million dollars?

25
00:00:45,927 --> 00:00:47,390
It's not a magic number.

26
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:49,439
But it's a powerful milestone.

27
00:00:49,619 --> 00:00:51,807
Invested and withdrawn carefully,

28
00:00:51,807 --> 00:00:53,994
using the four percent guideline,

29
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:56,911
a million dollars could support around forty

30
00:00:56,911 --> 00:00:59,298
thousand dollars a year of spending,

31
00:00:59,298 --> 00:01:00,822
before Social Security.

32
00:01:01,002 --> 00:01:02,031
For many people,

33
00:01:02,031 --> 00:01:03,638
that's financial freedom.

34
00:01:03,988 --> 00:01:06,069
We'll look at the whole journey from

35
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:06,820
three angles.

36
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,334
First, the numbers:

37
00:01:08,334 --> 00:01:10,089
how long it really takes,

38
00:01:10,089 --> 00:01:11,563
and why it speeds up.

39
00:01:11,743 --> 00:01:13,339
Second, the psychology:

40
00:01:13,339 --> 00:01:15,837
the mental challenges at each stage,

41
00:01:15,837 --> 00:01:17,780
and what keeps people going.

42
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:19,703
And third, the playbook:

43
00:01:19,703 --> 00:01:22,389
nine stages, from broke to a million,

44
00:01:22,389 --> 00:01:24,566
with the exact moves for each.

45
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:26,575
Stay for the stage I call the

46
00:01:26,575 --> 00:01:27,262
long middle.

47
00:01:27,442 --> 00:01:29,120
It's where most people quit,

48
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,517
and it's exactly where the magic starts.

49
00:01:31,697 --> 00:01:33,276
Let's start with the numbers.

50
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:36,964
Household data comes from the Federal Reserve's

51
00:01:36,964 --> 00:01:39,876
Survey of Consumer Finances and Survey of

52
00:01:39,876 --> 00:01:42,646
Household Economics and Decisionmaking.

53
00:01:42,826 --> 00:01:45,942
Retirement plan data comes from Fidelity and

54
00:01:45,942 --> 00:01:47,145
Vanguard reports.

55
00:01:47,325 --> 00:01:51,075
The four percent guideline comes from William

56
00:01:51,075 --> 00:01:52,991
Bengen's 1994 research.

57
00:01:53,171 --> 00:01:55,857
Growth examples use stated math at a

58
00:01:55,857 --> 00:01:58,544
seven percent average yearly return,

59
00:01:58,544 --> 00:02:00,857
an illustration, not a promise.

60
00:02:01,037 --> 00:02:02,403
This is education,

61
00:02:02,403 --> 00:02:03,997
not financial advice.

62
00:02:04,347 --> 00:02:05,125
Angle one.

63
00:02:05,305 --> 00:02:06,002
The numbers.

64
00:02:06,182 --> 00:02:07,567
How long does it take to go

65
00:02:07,567 --> 00:02:08,748
from zero to a million?

66
00:02:09,098 --> 00:02:12,124
Let's assume you've already cleared high interest

67
00:02:12,124 --> 00:02:14,285
debt and built your emergency fund,

68
00:02:14,285 --> 00:02:16,447
and now you're investing from zero.

69
00:02:16,627 --> 00:02:19,142
At a seven percent average yearly return,

70
00:02:19,142 --> 00:02:21,534
investing five hundred dollars a month,

71
00:02:21,534 --> 00:02:23,865
you could reach one million dollars in

72
00:02:23,865 --> 00:02:25,276
about thirty six years.

73
00:02:25,456 --> 00:02:27,522
At one thousand dollars a month,

74
00:02:27,522 --> 00:02:29,136
about twenty eight years.

75
00:02:29,486 --> 00:02:31,545
At fifteen hundred dollars a month,

76
00:02:31,545 --> 00:02:33,016
about twenty three years.

77
00:02:33,196 --> 00:02:35,324
And at two thousand dollars a month,

78
00:02:35,324 --> 00:02:36,447
about twenty years.

79
00:02:36,627 --> 00:02:38,546
Double your monthly investment,

80
00:02:38,546 --> 00:02:40,713
and you cut the timeline by roughly

81
00:02:40,713 --> 00:02:41,456
eight years.

82
00:02:41,636 --> 00:02:43,935
That's why growing your income and savings

83
00:02:43,935 --> 00:02:45,085
rate matters so much.

84
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:47,896
Years to reach one million dollars from

85
00:02:47,896 --> 00:02:50,357
zero, at a seven percent average yearly

86
00:02:50,357 --> 00:02:50,798
return.

87
00:02:51,148 --> 00:02:53,309
Now here's the most important chart in

88
00:02:53,309 --> 00:02:53,935
this video.

89
00:02:54,115 --> 00:02:56,833
Investing one thousand dollars a month at

90
00:02:56,833 --> 00:02:59,817
seven percent, the first one hundred thousand

91
00:02:59,817 --> 00:03:02,602
dollars takes about six point seven years.

92
00:03:02,782 --> 00:03:05,210
The second hundred thousand takes about four

93
00:03:05,210 --> 00:03:06,149
and a half years.

94
00:03:06,329 --> 00:03:08,337
The third, about three and a half.

95
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:10,591
And the tenth hundred thousand,

96
00:03:10,591 --> 00:03:12,555
the one that takes you from nine

97
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:14,397
hundred thousand to a million,

98
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:16,362
takes only about sixteen months.

99
00:03:16,542 --> 00:03:18,724
Same contribution every month.

100
00:03:18,904 --> 00:03:20,636
The difference is that your money is

101
00:03:20,636 --> 00:03:21,935
now doing most of the work.

102
00:03:22,115 --> 00:03:23,125
That's compounding.

103
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:25,975
Investing one thousand dollars a month at

104
00:03:25,975 --> 00:03:28,293
seven percent a year for thirty years.

105
00:03:28,642 --> 00:03:31,108
After thirty years at one thousand dollars

106
00:03:31,108 --> 00:03:33,046
a month, you could have about one

107
00:03:33,046 --> 00:03:34,807
point two two million dollars.

108
00:03:34,987 --> 00:03:37,611
You'd have contributed three hundred sixty thousand.

109
00:03:37,791 --> 00:03:40,299
The other roughly eight hundred sixty thousand

110
00:03:40,299 --> 00:03:40,845
is growth.

111
00:03:41,025 --> 00:03:42,219
In the later years,

112
00:03:42,219 --> 00:03:44,356
your portfolio can grow by more in

113
00:03:44,356 --> 00:03:46,493
a good year than you contribute in

114
00:03:46,493 --> 00:03:47,561
an entire decade.

115
00:03:47,911 --> 00:03:49,491
This is why Charlie Munger,

116
00:03:49,491 --> 00:03:52,007
Warren Buffett's longtime business partner,

117
00:03:52,007 --> 00:03:54,640
reportedly said the first hundred thousand is

118
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:55,342
the hardest.

119
00:03:55,522 --> 00:03:56,375
At the start,

120
00:03:56,375 --> 00:03:57,228
it's all you.

121
00:03:57,408 --> 00:03:58,175
By the end,

122
00:03:58,175 --> 00:03:59,777
it's mostly your money.

123
00:03:59,957 --> 00:04:02,345
The hardest part of becoming a millionaire

124
00:04:02,345 --> 00:04:03,312
is the beginning.

125
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:04,765
How common is it?

126
00:04:04,945 --> 00:04:08,056
Fidelity has reported hundreds of thousands of

127
00:04:08,056 --> 00:04:10,897
401k accounts with balances of one million

128
00:04:10,897 --> 00:04:13,061
dollars or more in recent years,

129
00:04:13,061 --> 00:04:15,563
and many of those savers had ordinary

130
00:04:15,563 --> 00:04:18,471
incomes and simply contributed for decades.

131
00:04:18,651 --> 00:04:21,495
Becoming a millionaire through steady investing is

132
00:04:21,495 --> 00:04:23,713
far more common than most people think.

133
00:04:24,062 --> 00:04:26,380
One more number to keep in mind:

134
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:27,104
inflation.

135
00:04:27,284 --> 00:04:29,640
A million dollars in thirty years won't

136
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:31,452
buy what a million buys today.

137
00:04:31,632 --> 00:04:33,316
At three percent inflation,

138
00:04:33,316 --> 00:04:35,560
it might buy what about four hundred

139
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:36,683
thousand buys now.

140
00:04:36,863 --> 00:04:38,442
That's not a reason to give up.

141
00:04:38,622 --> 00:04:40,689
It's a reason to start sooner and

142
00:04:40,689 --> 00:04:43,509
keep increasing your contributions over time.

143
00:04:43,859 --> 00:04:46,251
Let's put the milestones on a timeline.

144
00:04:46,431 --> 00:04:49,207
Investing one thousand dollars a month at

145
00:04:49,207 --> 00:04:51,509
seven percent, starting from zero:

146
00:04:51,509 --> 00:04:54,489
one hundred thousand dollars after about six

147
00:04:54,489 --> 00:04:55,707
point seven years.

148
00:04:55,887 --> 00:04:58,873
Two hundred fifty thousand after about thirteen

149
00:04:58,873 --> 00:04:59,254
years.

150
00:04:59,434 --> 00:05:02,511
Five hundred thousand after about twenty years.

151
00:05:02,691 --> 00:05:05,336
And one million after about twenty eight

152
00:05:05,336 --> 00:05:05,733
years.

153
00:05:06,082 --> 00:05:07,162
Look at the gaps.

154
00:05:07,342 --> 00:05:09,259
It takes about thirteen years to reach

155
00:05:09,259 --> 00:05:10,570
the first quarter million.

156
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:12,643
Then about seven years to double it

157
00:05:12,643 --> 00:05:13,617
to half a million.

158
00:05:13,797 --> 00:05:15,730
Then about eight years to double it

159
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:16,780
again to a million,

160
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:19,376
while you're contributing the same amount every

161
00:05:19,376 --> 00:05:19,707
month.

162
00:05:19,887 --> 00:05:21,962
Each doubling takes less of your own

163
00:05:21,962 --> 00:05:24,384
effort, because growth is carrying more of

164
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:24,902
the load.

165
00:05:25,252 --> 00:05:27,760
And the stages before investing matter too.

166
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:30,683
Clearing debt and building an emergency fund

167
00:05:30,683 --> 00:05:32,491
might take one to four years.

168
00:05:32,671 --> 00:05:34,244
Those years don't show up on the

169
00:05:34,244 --> 00:05:35,277
million dollar chart,

170
00:05:35,277 --> 00:05:37,292
but they're what make the chart possible.

171
00:05:37,472 --> 00:05:40,324
Without them, one emergency or one high

172
00:05:40,324 --> 00:05:43,614
interest balance can undo years of investing.

173
00:05:43,963 --> 00:05:45,342
So the math works,

174
00:05:45,342 --> 00:05:46,796
and it accelerates.

175
00:05:46,976 --> 00:05:48,761
The hard part is staying in the

176
00:05:48,761 --> 00:05:49,740
game long enough.

177
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:50,976
That's angle two.

178
00:05:51,325 --> 00:05:52,336
Angle two.

179
00:05:52,516 --> 00:05:53,711
The psychology.

180
00:05:53,891 --> 00:05:55,574
What it feels like at each part

181
00:05:55,574 --> 00:05:56,389
of the journey,

182
00:05:56,389 --> 00:05:57,583
and how to keep going.

183
00:05:57,933 --> 00:05:58,920
At the beginning,

184
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:00,488
the journey feels hopeless.

185
00:06:00,668 --> 00:06:03,259
You're paying off debt and saving small

186
00:06:03,259 --> 00:06:03,791
amounts.

187
00:06:03,971 --> 00:06:05,770
Your net worth might be negative.

188
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:07,113
It's easy to think,

189
00:06:07,113 --> 00:06:09,377
this will never work for someone like

190
00:06:09,377 --> 00:06:09,561
me.

191
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:11,533
The key here is quick wins:

192
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:13,459
a first five hundred dollars,

193
00:06:13,459 --> 00:06:14,919
a first card paid off.

194
00:06:15,099 --> 00:06:17,514
Small wins prove the system works.

195
00:06:17,863 --> 00:06:19,527
In the early building years,

196
00:06:19,527 --> 00:06:20,894
the problem is boredom.

197
00:06:21,074 --> 00:06:22,925
You're investing every month,

198
00:06:22,925 --> 00:06:24,777
but the balance barely moves.

199
00:06:24,957 --> 00:06:26,875
A market drop can wipe out a

200
00:06:26,875 --> 00:06:28,382
year of contributions.

201
00:06:28,562 --> 00:06:30,398
This is where the urge to do

202
00:06:30,398 --> 00:06:33,282
something exciting, like chasing a hot stock

203
00:06:33,282 --> 00:06:34,855
or crypto, is strongest.

204
00:06:35,035 --> 00:06:35,836
Resist it.

205
00:06:36,185 --> 00:06:37,790
Then comes the long middle,

206
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:40,226
roughly from one hundred thousand to five

207
00:06:40,226 --> 00:06:41,236
hundred thousand.

208
00:06:41,416 --> 00:06:43,575
Progress is real but slow.

209
00:06:43,755 --> 00:06:45,569
Life gets expensive:

210
00:06:45,569 --> 00:06:48,109
kids, homes, career changes.

211
00:06:48,289 --> 00:06:50,332
Lifestyle creep pulls hard.

212
00:06:50,512 --> 00:06:52,219
This is where most people stall.

213
00:06:52,399 --> 00:06:53,860
Not because they fail,

214
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:56,848
but because they slowly stop increasing their

215
00:06:56,848 --> 00:06:57,380
savings.

216
00:06:57,729 --> 00:06:59,668
And finally, the acceleration.

217
00:06:59,848 --> 00:07:01,607
After five hundred thousand,

218
00:07:01,607 --> 00:07:03,931
your money starts to grow faster than

219
00:07:03,931 --> 00:07:04,748
you can save.

220
00:07:04,928 --> 00:07:07,049
Good years can add more than your

221
00:07:07,049 --> 00:07:08,399
annual contributions.

222
00:07:08,579 --> 00:07:10,690
The challenge here is different:

223
00:07:10,690 --> 00:07:12,866
fear of losing what you've built,

224
00:07:12,866 --> 00:07:15,240
and the temptation to take big risks

225
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:16,427
or stop too early.

226
00:07:16,777 --> 00:07:19,166
Notice that the hardest feelings come at

227
00:07:19,166 --> 00:07:20,121
the wrong times.

228
00:07:20,301 --> 00:07:22,937
Hopelessness hits when you've barely started,

229
00:07:22,937 --> 00:07:25,398
and that's when the system needs patience.

230
00:07:25,578 --> 00:07:28,279
Boredom hits when compounding is still small,

231
00:07:28,279 --> 00:07:30,501
and that's when it needs consistency.

232
00:07:30,681 --> 00:07:32,474
Fear hits when you have the most

233
00:07:32,474 --> 00:07:34,324
to lose, and that's when it needs

234
00:07:34,324 --> 00:07:34,605
calm.

235
00:07:34,785 --> 00:07:37,076
Knowing these feelings are coming makes them

236
00:07:37,076 --> 00:07:38,221
much easier to handle.

237
00:07:38,571 --> 00:07:40,784
Research in behavioral finance,

238
00:07:40,784 --> 00:07:43,781
including work by Daniel Kahneman and Amos

239
00:07:43,781 --> 00:07:45,565
Tversky on loss aversion,

240
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:48,634
suggests that losses can feel roughly twice

241
00:07:48,634 --> 00:07:51,203
as painful as equal gains feel good.

242
00:07:51,383 --> 00:07:53,493
That's why market drops feel so much

243
00:07:53,493 --> 00:07:54,959
bigger than market rises.

244
00:07:55,139 --> 00:07:55,963
It's normal.

245
00:07:56,143 --> 00:07:57,670
The trick is not to let that

246
00:07:57,670 --> 00:07:59,197
feeling make your decisions.

247
00:07:59,546 --> 00:08:02,414
So build rules for yourself in advance.

248
00:08:02,594 --> 00:08:04,042
When the market drops,

249
00:08:04,042 --> 00:08:05,160
I keep investing.

250
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:06,658
When I get a raise,

251
00:08:06,658 --> 00:08:08,254
half goes to my future.

252
00:08:08,434 --> 00:08:10,268
When I feel like quitting,

253
00:08:10,268 --> 00:08:11,255
I read my why.

254
00:08:11,435 --> 00:08:13,744
Rules made in calm moments protect you

255
00:08:13,744 --> 00:08:14,837
in emotional ones.

256
00:08:15,186 --> 00:08:16,069
Mindset one.

257
00:08:16,249 --> 00:08:17,688
Focus on the system,

258
00:08:17,688 --> 00:08:18,768
not the number.

259
00:08:18,948 --> 00:08:20,527
You can't control the market.

260
00:08:20,707 --> 00:08:22,676
You can control your savings rate,

261
00:08:22,676 --> 00:08:24,992
your fees, your behavior and your income

262
00:08:24,992 --> 00:08:25,398
growth.

263
00:08:25,578 --> 00:08:26,843
Put your energy there.

264
00:08:27,193 --> 00:08:28,331
Mindset two.

265
00:08:28,511 --> 00:08:30,369
Measure yearly, not daily.

266
00:08:30,549 --> 00:08:33,081
Checking your balance every day makes market

267
00:08:33,081 --> 00:08:34,519
noise feel like a crisis.

268
00:08:34,699 --> 00:08:35,953
Check once a quarter.

269
00:08:36,133 --> 00:08:37,898
Measure progress once a year.

270
00:08:38,247 --> 00:08:39,420
Mindset three.

271
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:41,156
Celebrate milestones.

272
00:08:41,336 --> 00:08:42,822
Your first ten thousand.

273
00:08:43,002 --> 00:08:44,523
Your first hundred thousand.

274
00:08:44,703 --> 00:08:47,097
The year your portfolio grew more than

275
00:08:47,097 --> 00:08:48,105
you contributed.

276
00:08:48,285 --> 00:08:50,560
Celebrating keeps the journey meaningful.

277
00:08:50,910 --> 00:08:51,839
Mindset four.

278
00:08:52,019 --> 00:08:54,794
Never interrupt compounding unnecessarily.

279
00:08:54,974 --> 00:08:57,872
Cashing out a retirement account when changing

280
00:08:57,872 --> 00:09:00,329
jobs, or selling everything in a crash,

281
00:09:00,329 --> 00:09:01,778
can set you back years.

282
00:09:01,958 --> 00:09:03,258
Protect the snowball.

283
00:09:03,607 --> 00:09:04,861
And mindset five.

284
00:09:05,041 --> 00:09:06,075
Remember why.

285
00:09:06,255 --> 00:09:08,240
A million dollars isn't the goal.

286
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:09,430
Freedom is.

287
00:09:09,610 --> 00:09:11,096
Security for your family.

288
00:09:11,276 --> 00:09:12,553
Time for what matters.

289
00:09:12,733 --> 00:09:14,539
Write down your why and read it

290
00:09:14,539 --> 00:09:16,170
when the journey feels long.

291
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:20,290
In 1996, researchers Thomas Stanley and William

292
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:23,660
Danko published The Millionaire Next Door.

293
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:25,873
After surveying wealthy Americans,

294
00:09:25,873 --> 00:09:27,787
they found something surprising.

295
00:09:27,967 --> 00:09:30,045
Many millionaires didn't look rich.

296
00:09:30,225 --> 00:09:32,308
They lived in ordinary neighborhoods,

297
00:09:32,308 --> 00:09:33,434
drove ordinary cars,

298
00:09:33,434 --> 00:09:35,460
and had built their wealth slowly by

299
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:37,937
spending less than they earned and investing

300
00:09:37,937 --> 00:09:38,782
the difference.

301
00:09:39,132 --> 00:09:41,778
Since then, the tools for ordinary people

302
00:09:41,778 --> 00:09:43,392
have improved enormously.

303
00:09:43,572 --> 00:09:48,019
The 401k became widespread in the 1980s.

304
00:09:48,199 --> 00:09:50,793
Low cost index funds became easy to

305
00:09:50,793 --> 00:09:51,090
buy.

306
00:09:51,270 --> 00:09:54,897
Automatic enrollment became more common after the

307
00:09:54,897 --> 00:09:57,191
Pension Protection Act of 2006.

308
00:09:57,371 --> 00:09:59,950
Today, a regular worker can build a

309
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:03,192
diversified portfolio for a tiny fraction of

310
00:10:03,192 --> 00:10:05,034
a percent in yearly fees.

311
00:10:05,384 --> 00:10:06,974
That's why this playbook works.

312
00:10:07,154 --> 00:10:08,269
It's not a secret.

313
00:10:08,449 --> 00:10:10,947
It's the same pattern Stanley and Danko

314
00:10:10,947 --> 00:10:12,100
found decades ago,

315
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:13,894
made easier by modern tools.

316
00:10:14,074 --> 00:10:15,606
Spend less than you earn.

317
00:10:15,786 --> 00:10:17,284
Avoid expensive debt.

318
00:10:17,464 --> 00:10:20,517
Invest steadily in low cost funds.

319
00:10:20,697 --> 00:10:21,765
And give it time.

320
00:10:22,116 --> 00:10:23,079
Quick pause.

321
00:10:23,259 --> 00:10:24,733
If you're on this journey,

322
00:10:24,733 --> 00:10:25,754
or ready to start,

323
00:10:25,754 --> 00:10:27,172
subscribe to the channel.

324
00:10:27,352 --> 00:10:29,086
It's free, and it's the best way

325
00:10:29,086 --> 00:10:31,090
to follow the whole playbook with me.

326
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:33,139
Now, the nine stages.

327
00:10:33,490 --> 00:10:34,628
Before the stages,

328
00:10:34,628 --> 00:10:36,652
here's a quick way to find where

329
00:10:36,652 --> 00:10:37,158
you are.

330
00:10:37,338 --> 00:10:39,115
Answer these questions in order.

331
00:10:39,295 --> 00:10:41,129
The first no is your stage.

332
00:10:41,479 --> 00:10:42,361
Question one.

333
00:10:42,541 --> 00:10:43,677
In the last month,

334
00:10:43,677 --> 00:10:45,823
did your total debt go down rather

335
00:10:45,823 --> 00:10:47,779
than up, and are all your bills

336
00:10:47,779 --> 00:10:48,474
on autopay?

337
00:10:48,654 --> 00:10:50,685
If no, you're in stage one.

338
00:10:50,865 --> 00:10:51,690
Question two.

339
00:10:51,870 --> 00:10:53,596
Do you have at least one thousand

340
00:10:53,596 --> 00:10:55,585
dollars in a separate savings account?

341
00:10:55,765 --> 00:10:57,785
If no, you're in stage two.

342
00:10:58,135 --> 00:10:59,168
Question three.

343
00:10:59,348 --> 00:11:01,145
Are you free of debt above about

344
00:11:01,145 --> 00:11:02,436
eight percent interest?

345
00:11:02,616 --> 00:11:04,346
If no, stage three.

346
00:11:04,526 --> 00:11:05,432
Question four.

347
00:11:05,612 --> 00:11:07,484
Do you have three to six months

348
00:11:07,484 --> 00:11:09,176
of essential expenses saved?

349
00:11:09,356 --> 00:11:11,155
If no, stage four.

350
00:11:11,335 --> 00:11:12,311
Question five.

351
00:11:12,491 --> 00:11:15,050
Are you investing at least fifteen percent,

352
00:11:15,050 --> 00:11:17,669
with at least ten thousand dollars invested?

353
00:11:17,849 --> 00:11:19,648
If no, stage five.

354
00:11:19,999 --> 00:11:22,536
After that, your stage is simply your

355
00:11:22,536 --> 00:11:23,702
invested balance.

356
00:11:23,882 --> 00:11:26,051
Under one hundred thousand,

357
00:11:26,051 --> 00:11:26,854
stage six.

358
00:11:27,034 --> 00:11:29,419
One hundred to five hundred thousand,

359
00:11:29,419 --> 00:11:30,192
stage seven.

360
00:11:30,372 --> 00:11:32,915
Five hundred thousand to one million,

361
00:11:32,915 --> 00:11:33,739
stage eight.

362
00:11:33,919 --> 00:11:35,225
Over a million,

363
00:11:35,225 --> 00:11:36,183
stage nine.

364
00:11:36,363 --> 00:11:38,581
Pause the video and find yours.

365
00:11:38,931 --> 00:11:39,848
Angle three.

366
00:11:40,028 --> 00:11:40,829
The playbook.

367
00:11:41,009 --> 00:11:43,510
Nine stages from broke to one million

368
00:11:43,510 --> 00:11:44,051
dollars.

369
00:11:44,231 --> 00:11:46,379
Each stage has one main goal.

370
00:11:46,729 --> 00:11:47,785
Stage 1.

371
00:11:47,965 --> 00:11:49,149
Stop the bleeding.

372
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:50,486
Stage one.

373
00:11:50,666 --> 00:11:51,920
Stop the bleeding.

374
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:54,112
The goal is to stop going further

375
00:11:54,112 --> 00:11:54,538
behind.

376
00:11:54,718 --> 00:11:56,506
Track your spending for one month.

377
00:11:56,686 --> 00:11:57,905
Find the leaks:

378
00:11:57,905 --> 00:12:00,912
overdraft fees, unused subscriptions,

379
00:12:00,912 --> 00:12:04,082
late payments, high interest borrowing.

380
00:12:04,262 --> 00:12:06,638
Set up autopay for minimum payments so

381
00:12:06,638 --> 00:12:07,826
you never miss one.

382
00:12:08,176 --> 00:12:10,509
If you're using payday loans or carrying

383
00:12:10,509 --> 00:12:13,133
overdrafts, getting out of those comes first.

384
00:12:13,313 --> 00:12:16,644
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has reported

385
00:12:16,644 --> 00:12:19,283
that typical payday loans can carry annual

386
00:12:19,283 --> 00:12:22,299
percentage rates of almost four hundred percent.

387
00:12:22,479 --> 00:12:25,227
Escaping that cycle is the first big

388
00:12:25,227 --> 00:12:25,533
win.

389
00:12:25,883 --> 00:12:28,170
You don't need perfection at this stage.

390
00:12:28,350 --> 00:12:29,976
You need a month where you don't

391
00:12:29,976 --> 00:12:30,637
add new debt.

392
00:12:30,817 --> 00:12:32,106
That's the turning point.

393
00:12:32,456 --> 00:12:33,992
How do you know stage one is

394
00:12:33,992 --> 00:12:34,267
done?

395
00:12:34,447 --> 00:12:36,167
You've tracked a full month,

396
00:12:36,167 --> 00:12:38,071
every bill is on autopay for at

397
00:12:38,071 --> 00:12:39,177
least the minimum,

398
00:12:39,177 --> 00:12:41,450
and your total debt went down instead

399
00:12:41,450 --> 00:12:41,819
of up.

400
00:12:41,999 --> 00:12:44,202
The most common mistake here is waiting

401
00:12:44,202 --> 00:12:46,179
for a perfect plan before starting.

402
00:12:46,359 --> 00:12:47,276
Start messy.

403
00:12:47,456 --> 00:12:48,489
Fix it as you go.

404
00:12:48,839 --> 00:12:50,046
Stage 2.

405
00:12:50,226 --> 00:12:52,954
Build a $1,000 starter fund.

406
00:12:53,305 --> 00:12:54,547
Stage two.

407
00:12:54,727 --> 00:12:57,067
Build a starter emergency fund of one

408
00:12:57,067 --> 00:12:59,659
thousand dollars in a separate high yield

409
00:12:59,659 --> 00:13:00,671
savings account.

410
00:13:00,851 --> 00:13:02,772
Sell things you don't use,

411
00:13:02,772 --> 00:13:04,471
pick up an extra shift,

412
00:13:04,471 --> 00:13:05,948
or use a tax refund.

413
00:13:06,128 --> 00:13:08,171
Automate a transfer every payday.

414
00:13:08,522 --> 00:13:10,931
This small cushion means the next car

415
00:13:10,931 --> 00:13:13,276
repair or medical bill doesn't go on

416
00:13:13,276 --> 00:13:14,187
a credit card.

417
00:13:14,367 --> 00:13:16,632
It breaks the cycle of every surprise

418
00:13:16,632 --> 00:13:17,734
becoming new debt.

419
00:13:18,084 --> 00:13:19,121
At the same time,

420
00:13:19,121 --> 00:13:21,746
if your employer offers a retirement match,

421
00:13:21,746 --> 00:13:23,455
contribute enough to get it,

422
00:13:23,455 --> 00:13:24,005
even now.

423
00:13:24,185 --> 00:13:26,344
The match is too valuable to skip.

424
00:13:26,694 --> 00:13:28,280
How do you know stage two is

425
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:28,563
done?

426
00:13:28,743 --> 00:13:31,034
One thousand dollars sits in a separate

427
00:13:31,034 --> 00:13:33,442
account, and you've resisted using it for

428
00:13:33,442 --> 00:13:35,616
anything that isn't a real emergency.

429
00:13:35,796 --> 00:13:38,070
The common mistake is keeping it in

430
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:40,475
checking, where it slowly disappears.

431
00:13:40,655 --> 00:13:42,030
Separate it, name it,

432
00:13:42,030 --> 00:13:43,012
and protect it.

433
00:13:43,362 --> 00:13:44,453
Stage 3.

434
00:13:44,633 --> 00:13:45,968
Kill high interest debt.

435
00:13:46,318 --> 00:13:47,397
Stage three.

436
00:13:47,577 --> 00:13:49,528
Pay off all high interest debt,

437
00:13:49,528 --> 00:13:52,233
usually anything above about eight percent.

438
00:13:52,413 --> 00:13:54,770
Credit cards, personal loans,

439
00:13:54,770 --> 00:13:55,745
store cards.

440
00:13:55,925 --> 00:13:58,356
List them by interest rate and attack

441
00:13:58,356 --> 00:13:59,539
the highest first,

442
00:13:59,539 --> 00:14:01,576
or by balance if you need quick

443
00:14:01,576 --> 00:14:01,904
wins.

444
00:14:02,254 --> 00:14:03,911
Every debt you pay off frees up

445
00:14:03,911 --> 00:14:04,552
its payment.

446
00:14:04,732 --> 00:14:06,741
Roll that payment into the next debt.

447
00:14:06,921 --> 00:14:07,559
By the end,

448
00:14:07,559 --> 00:14:10,114
you might be sending several hundred dollars

449
00:14:10,114 --> 00:14:11,507
a month at the last one.

450
00:14:11,857 --> 00:14:13,274
When this stage ends,

451
00:14:13,274 --> 00:14:15,096
something powerful happens.

452
00:14:15,276 --> 00:14:17,721
All those payments become money you can

453
00:14:17,721 --> 00:14:18,724
save and invest.

454
00:14:18,904 --> 00:14:20,669
Your income finally works for you.

455
00:14:21,019 --> 00:14:22,700
How do you know stage three is

456
00:14:22,700 --> 00:14:22,981
done?

457
00:14:23,161 --> 00:14:25,944
No balances above about eight percent interest

458
00:14:25,944 --> 00:14:28,304
remain, and you're not adding new ones.

459
00:14:28,484 --> 00:14:30,603
The common mistake is clearing the cards

460
00:14:30,603 --> 00:14:32,246
and then running them up again.

461
00:14:32,426 --> 00:14:34,864
Keep one card for small purchases you

462
00:14:34,864 --> 00:14:35,655
pay in full,

463
00:14:35,655 --> 00:14:37,104
and put the rest away.

464
00:14:37,455 --> 00:14:38,430
Stage 4.

465
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:40,862
Build the full emergency fund.

466
00:14:41,212 --> 00:14:42,152
Stage four.

467
00:14:42,332 --> 00:14:44,822
Grow your emergency fund to three to

468
00:14:44,822 --> 00:14:47,104
six months of essential expenses.

469
00:14:47,284 --> 00:14:49,733
Send your old debt payments here first.

470
00:14:49,913 --> 00:14:52,746
This fund protects everything you'll build next.

471
00:14:53,097 --> 00:14:54,492
With this in place,

472
00:14:54,492 --> 00:14:56,402
a job loss or a big repair

473
00:14:56,402 --> 00:14:59,194
won't force you to sell investments at

474
00:14:59,194 --> 00:15:01,177
a bad time or borrow again.

475
00:15:01,357 --> 00:15:02,483
It's your foundation.

476
00:15:02,834 --> 00:15:04,585
At the end of stage four,

477
00:15:04,585 --> 00:15:06,967
your net worth may still be small.

478
00:15:07,147 --> 00:15:09,167
But your financial life is stable.

479
00:15:09,347 --> 00:15:11,487
That's a huge achievement that many people

480
00:15:11,487 --> 00:15:12,098
never reach.

481
00:15:12,449 --> 00:15:14,092
How do you know stage four is

482
00:15:14,092 --> 00:15:14,376
done?

483
00:15:14,556 --> 00:15:17,423
Your essential expenses are covered for three

484
00:15:17,423 --> 00:15:18,315
to six months,

485
00:15:18,315 --> 00:15:20,990
and you have sinking funds for predictable

486
00:15:20,990 --> 00:15:22,009
irregular costs.

487
00:15:22,189 --> 00:15:24,968
The common mistake is investing the emergency

488
00:15:24,968 --> 00:15:26,079
fund to earn more.

489
00:15:26,259 --> 00:15:27,222
Keep it safe.

490
00:15:27,402 --> 00:15:29,028
Its job is protection,

491
00:15:29,028 --> 00:15:29,841
not growth.

492
00:15:30,191 --> 00:15:31,073
Stage 5.

493
00:15:31,253 --> 00:15:35,096
Invest 15% and reach $10,000.

494
00:15:35,446 --> 00:15:36,432
Stage five.

495
00:15:36,612 --> 00:15:39,183
Raise your retirement investing to at least

496
00:15:39,183 --> 00:15:41,036
fifteen percent of your income,

497
00:15:41,036 --> 00:15:42,232
including the match.

498
00:15:42,412 --> 00:15:44,549
Use low cost index funds or a

499
00:15:44,549 --> 00:15:45,802
target date fund.

500
00:15:45,982 --> 00:15:48,466
Open a Roth IRA if you qualify.

501
00:15:48,817 --> 00:15:51,543
Your first goal is ten thousand dollars

502
00:15:51,543 --> 00:15:52,172
invested.

503
00:15:52,352 --> 00:15:54,128
It might take a year or two.

504
00:15:54,308 --> 00:15:55,527
It will feel slow.

505
00:15:55,707 --> 00:15:57,646
But this is where the engine starts.

506
00:15:57,996 --> 00:16:01,159
Turn on automatic contribution increases if your

507
00:16:01,159 --> 00:16:02,280
plan offers them,

508
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,455
so your rate rises by one percent

509
00:16:04,455 --> 00:16:06,366
each year without any effort.

510
00:16:06,717 --> 00:16:08,311
How do you know stage five is

511
00:16:08,311 --> 00:16:08,586
done?

512
00:16:08,766 --> 00:16:10,815
Ten thousand dollars invested,

513
00:16:10,815 --> 00:16:13,479
fifteen percent going in automatically,

514
00:16:13,479 --> 00:16:15,801
and low cost funds you understand.

515
00:16:15,981 --> 00:16:19,244
The common mistake is constantly switching investments.

516
00:16:19,424 --> 00:16:20,422
Pick a simple,

517
00:16:20,422 --> 00:16:22,919
diversified mix and leave it alone.

518
00:16:23,269 --> 00:16:24,534
Stage 6.

519
00:16:24,714 --> 00:16:26,688
Reach $100,000.

520
00:16:27,038 --> 00:16:28,442
Stage six.

521
00:16:28,622 --> 00:16:31,351
Reach one hundred thousand dollars invested.

522
00:16:31,531 --> 00:16:33,512
At one thousand dollars a month,

523
00:16:33,512 --> 00:16:35,556
it takes about six to seven years

524
00:16:35,556 --> 00:16:36,175
from zero.

525
00:16:36,355 --> 00:16:38,181
This is the hardest milestone,

526
00:16:38,181 --> 00:16:40,251
because most of it comes from your

527
00:16:40,251 --> 00:16:41,347
own contributions.

528
00:16:41,697 --> 00:16:43,682
Speed it up with income growth.

529
00:16:43,862 --> 00:16:45,000
Ask for raises,

530
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,958
build skills, change jobs when it makes

531
00:16:47,958 --> 00:16:48,413
sense.

532
00:16:48,593 --> 00:16:49,851
Use the raise rule:

533
00:16:49,851 --> 00:16:52,366
half of every raise goes to investing.

534
00:16:52,717 --> 00:16:55,902
Avoid the big mistakes that reset progress:

535
00:16:55,902 --> 00:16:58,124
cashing out old 401k accounts,

536
00:16:58,124 --> 00:17:00,347
buying a car you can't afford,

537
00:17:00,347 --> 00:17:02,643
or panic selling in a downturn.

538
00:17:02,993 --> 00:17:04,864
How do you know stage six is

539
00:17:04,864 --> 00:17:05,199
done?

540
00:17:05,379 --> 00:17:08,351
Your investments cross one hundred thousand dollars.

541
00:17:08,531 --> 00:17:09,285
Celebrate it.

542
00:17:09,465 --> 00:17:12,599
The common mistake here is lifestyle creep,

543
00:17:12,599 --> 00:17:15,660
raising spending as income grows until the

544
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:17,627
savings rate quietly falls.

545
00:17:17,807 --> 00:17:19,572
Watch your rate every year.

546
00:17:19,922 --> 00:17:21,094
Stage 7.

547
00:17:21,274 --> 00:17:23,390
The long middle:

548
00:17:23,390 --> 00:17:25,373
$100K to $500K.

549
00:17:25,723 --> 00:17:26,860
Stage seven.

550
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:27,783
The long middle.

551
00:17:27,963 --> 00:17:30,315
From one hundred thousand to five hundred

552
00:17:30,315 --> 00:17:30,831
thousand.

553
00:17:31,011 --> 00:17:32,642
At one thousand a month,

554
00:17:32,642 --> 00:17:35,156
it takes roughly thirteen more years.

555
00:17:35,336 --> 00:17:37,681
Life gets busy and expensive.

556
00:17:37,861 --> 00:17:39,858
This is where discipline matters most.

557
00:17:40,208 --> 00:17:43,095
Keep fixed costs under control as income

558
00:17:43,095 --> 00:17:43,528
rises.

559
00:17:43,708 --> 00:17:45,920
Buy a home you can comfortably afford,

560
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:47,783
not the maximum the bank offers.

561
00:17:47,963 --> 00:17:49,391
Keep cars longer.

562
00:17:49,571 --> 00:17:52,183
Protect your savings rate from lifestyle creep.

563
00:17:52,534 --> 00:17:54,353
And protect your family:

564
00:17:54,353 --> 00:17:57,309
term life insurance if people depend on

565
00:17:57,309 --> 00:17:59,279
you, disability insurance,

566
00:17:59,279 --> 00:18:01,856
a will, and updated beneficiaries.

567
00:18:02,036 --> 00:18:04,219
You now have something worth protecting.

568
00:18:04,569 --> 00:18:06,370
How do you know stage seven is

569
00:18:06,370 --> 00:18:06,670
done?

570
00:18:06,850 --> 00:18:09,335
Five hundred thousand dollars invested.

571
00:18:09,515 --> 00:18:12,021
The common mistake in this stage is

572
00:18:12,021 --> 00:18:15,458
a big lifestyle decision that permanently raises

573
00:18:15,458 --> 00:18:18,251
fixed costs, like an oversized house or

574
00:18:18,251 --> 00:18:20,184
two expensive car payments.

575
00:18:20,364 --> 00:18:22,195
One decision like that can add a

576
00:18:22,195 --> 00:18:23,510
decade to your journey.

577
00:18:23,861 --> 00:18:24,906
Stage 8.

578
00:18:25,086 --> 00:18:29,021
The acceleration: $500K to $1M.

579
00:18:29,372 --> 00:18:30,591
Stage eight.

580
00:18:30,771 --> 00:18:32,129
The acceleration.

581
00:18:32,309 --> 00:18:34,619
From five hundred thousand to a million.

582
00:18:34,799 --> 00:18:36,700
At one thousand a month and seven

583
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:39,118
percent, this stage might take about eight

584
00:18:39,118 --> 00:18:41,307
years, less time than the long middle,

585
00:18:41,307 --> 00:18:43,437
even though the dollar gap is bigger.

586
00:18:43,788 --> 00:18:47,236
Now, investment growth can exceed your contributions.

587
00:18:47,416 --> 00:18:49,519
A seven percent year on six hundred

588
00:18:49,519 --> 00:18:52,283
thousand dollars is forty two thousand dollars

589
00:18:52,283 --> 00:18:52,884
of growth.

590
00:18:53,064 --> 00:18:55,328
Keep contributing, but let the money do

591
00:18:55,328 --> 00:18:56,373
the heavy lifting.

592
00:18:56,723 --> 00:18:57,879
As you get closer,

593
00:18:57,879 --> 00:18:59,614
review your investment mix.

594
00:18:59,794 --> 00:19:02,378
Many people gradually add more stable assets,

595
00:19:02,378 --> 00:19:04,560
like bonds, as they approach the point

596
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:06,225
where they'll need the money.

597
00:19:06,405 --> 00:19:08,327
A good plan reduces the risk of

598
00:19:08,327 --> 00:19:10,434
a big drop right before your goal.

599
00:19:10,784 --> 00:19:12,456
How do you know stage eight is

600
00:19:12,456 --> 00:19:12,734
done?

601
00:19:12,914 --> 00:19:14,331
You cross one million.

602
00:19:14,511 --> 00:19:16,824
The common mistakes here are taking big

603
00:19:16,824 --> 00:19:18,366
risks to get there faster,

604
00:19:18,366 --> 00:19:20,620
or panicking in a downturn and selling

605
00:19:20,620 --> 00:19:21,569
near the bottom.

606
00:19:21,749 --> 00:19:22,571
At this stage,

607
00:19:22,571 --> 00:19:25,154
protecting what you've built matters as much

608
00:19:25,154 --> 00:19:25,975
as growing it.

609
00:19:26,326 --> 00:19:27,336
Stage 9.

610
00:19:27,516 --> 00:19:30,093
Reach $1 million and protect it.

611
00:19:30,443 --> 00:19:31,430
Stage nine.

612
00:19:31,610 --> 00:19:33,281
One million dollars.

613
00:19:33,461 --> 00:19:35,590
Using the four percent guideline,

614
00:19:35,590 --> 00:19:38,558
it could support around forty thousand dollars

615
00:19:38,558 --> 00:19:39,783
a year of spending,

616
00:19:39,783 --> 00:19:41,461
more with Social Security.

617
00:19:41,641 --> 00:19:43,606
You may be able to work by

618
00:19:43,606 --> 00:19:44,890
choice, cut back,

619
00:19:44,890 --> 00:19:45,646
or retire.

620
00:19:45,996 --> 00:19:48,277
Now the focus shifts to protection and

621
00:19:48,277 --> 00:19:48,817
planning.

622
00:19:48,997 --> 00:19:51,480
A sustainable withdrawal strategy,

623
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:53,306
tax planning, healthcare,

624
00:19:53,306 --> 00:19:54,767
and estate planning.

625
00:19:54,947 --> 00:19:57,215
Many people benefit from a fee only

626
00:19:57,215 --> 00:19:59,289
fiduciary advisor at this stage.

627
00:19:59,639 --> 00:20:01,182
And think about what the money is

628
00:20:01,182 --> 00:20:01,369
for.

629
00:20:01,549 --> 00:20:03,971
Freedom, family, giving,

630
00:20:03,971 --> 00:20:05,182
experiences.

631
00:20:05,362 --> 00:20:07,204
Reaching a million isn't the end of

632
00:20:07,204 --> 00:20:07,835
the journey.

633
00:20:08,015 --> 00:20:09,856
It's the beginning of a life with

634
00:20:09,856 --> 00:20:10,581
more choices.

635
00:20:10,931 --> 00:20:12,926
And what's the common mistake after a

636
00:20:12,926 --> 00:20:13,357
million?

637
00:20:13,537 --> 00:20:15,035
Stopping all planning.

638
00:20:15,215 --> 00:20:18,152
A million dollars without a withdrawal plan,

639
00:20:18,152 --> 00:20:21,223
tax strategy and healthcare plan can disappear

640
00:20:21,223 --> 00:20:22,959
faster than people expect.

641
00:20:23,139 --> 00:20:24,544
The playbook doesn't end.

642
00:20:24,724 --> 00:20:27,209
It changes from building to preserving and

643
00:20:27,209 --> 00:20:27,742
enjoying.

644
00:20:28,092 --> 00:20:29,868
Let's look at three paths.

645
00:20:30,048 --> 00:20:31,743
First, the early starter.

646
00:20:31,923 --> 00:20:34,226
Mia starts at twenty three with debt

647
00:20:34,226 --> 00:20:35,058
from college.

648
00:20:35,238 --> 00:20:37,245
She clears it by twenty six,

649
00:20:37,245 --> 00:20:39,109
builds her emergency fund,

650
00:20:39,109 --> 00:20:41,906
and invests fifteen percent of a modest

651
00:20:41,906 --> 00:20:44,630
salary, increasing it with each raise.

652
00:20:44,810 --> 00:20:46,002
By her late fifties,

653
00:20:46,002 --> 00:20:48,148
she could reach a million dollars on

654
00:20:48,148 --> 00:20:49,280
an ordinary career.

655
00:20:49,630 --> 00:20:51,429
Second, the late starter.

656
00:20:51,609 --> 00:20:54,262
Andre is broke at thirty eight after

657
00:20:54,262 --> 00:20:54,999
a divorce.

658
00:20:55,179 --> 00:20:56,917
He rebuilds stage by stage,

659
00:20:56,917 --> 00:20:59,170
and by forty two he's investing two

660
00:20:59,170 --> 00:21:00,778
thousand dollars a month,

661
00:21:00,778 --> 00:21:03,353
about twenty five percent of his income.

662
00:21:03,533 --> 00:21:04,576
If he keeps it up,

663
00:21:04,576 --> 00:21:06,720
he could reach a million around sixty

664
00:21:06,720 --> 00:21:09,096
two, with his highest earning years doing

665
00:21:09,096 --> 00:21:10,139
the heavy lifting.

666
00:21:10,489 --> 00:21:12,021
Third, the high saver.

667
00:21:12,201 --> 00:21:13,254
Grace and Luis,

668
00:21:13,254 --> 00:21:15,781
both thirty, share one paid off car,

669
00:21:15,781 --> 00:21:18,728
rent modestly, and invest forty percent of

670
00:21:18,728 --> 00:21:20,272
their combined income,

671
00:21:20,272 --> 00:21:23,080
about two thousand five hundred a month.

672
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:24,082
At that pace,

673
00:21:24,082 --> 00:21:26,296
they could reach a million in under

674
00:21:26,296 --> 00:21:28,194
twenty years, by around fifty.

675
00:21:28,544 --> 00:21:30,668
Mia's biggest advantage was time.

676
00:21:30,848 --> 00:21:32,516
Starting in her twenties,

677
00:21:32,516 --> 00:21:35,852
even modest contributions had decades to compound.

678
00:21:36,032 --> 00:21:38,122
Her biggest challenge was patience.

679
00:21:38,302 --> 00:21:39,310
In her thirties,

680
00:21:39,310 --> 00:21:42,021
her balance felt small compared to friends'

681
00:21:42,021 --> 00:21:42,714
lifestyles.

682
00:21:42,894 --> 00:21:44,207
But by her late forties,

683
00:21:44,207 --> 00:21:46,451
her investments were growing by more each

684
00:21:46,451 --> 00:21:47,875
year than she contributed.

685
00:21:48,225 --> 00:21:50,582
Andre's biggest advantage was income.

686
00:21:50,762 --> 00:21:53,340
Starting later, he used his peak earning

687
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:56,176
years and catch up contributions after fifty

688
00:21:56,176 --> 00:21:57,530
to save aggressively.

689
00:21:57,710 --> 00:22:00,368
His biggest challenge was avoiding the urge

690
00:22:00,368 --> 00:22:02,099
to catch up with risky bets.

691
00:22:02,279 --> 00:22:03,635
He stuck to the plan,

692
00:22:03,635 --> 00:22:04,926
and the plan worked.

693
00:22:05,276 --> 00:22:07,964
Grace and Luis's biggest advantage was their

694
00:22:07,964 --> 00:22:08,759
savings rate.

695
00:22:08,939 --> 00:22:11,560
Their biggest challenge was social pressure from

696
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:13,962
friends who didn't understand why they lived

697
00:22:13,962 --> 00:22:14,453
modestly.

698
00:22:14,633 --> 00:22:16,011
Their answer was simple:

699
00:22:16,011 --> 00:22:17,791
we're buying our freedom early.

700
00:22:17,971 --> 00:22:19,909
By fifty, they may be able to

701
00:22:19,909 --> 00:22:21,779
work only on what they love.

702
00:22:22,130 --> 00:22:23,546
Different starting points.

703
00:22:23,726 --> 00:22:24,899
Different timelines.

704
00:22:25,079 --> 00:22:26,147
The same playbook.

705
00:22:26,327 --> 00:22:27,879
These are illustrations,

706
00:22:27,879 --> 00:22:30,530
not promises, and real returns will vary.

707
00:22:30,710 --> 00:22:32,571
But they show that a million dollars

708
00:22:32,571 --> 00:22:34,587
is reachable from very different lives.

709
00:22:34,938 --> 00:22:36,882
There are four things that can speed

710
00:22:36,882 --> 00:22:38,502
up the journey at every stage.

711
00:22:38,682 --> 00:22:39,797
Let's go through them.

712
00:22:40,147 --> 00:22:42,538
Accelerator one: income growth.

713
00:22:42,718 --> 00:22:45,762
Every raise, promotion or job change that

714
00:22:45,762 --> 00:22:47,172
increases your pay,

715
00:22:47,172 --> 00:22:49,325
combined with the raise rule,

716
00:22:49,325 --> 00:22:50,067
adds fuel.

717
00:22:50,247 --> 00:22:52,416
Going from one thousand to two thousand

718
00:22:52,416 --> 00:22:54,696
dollars a month invested cuts the journey

719
00:22:54,696 --> 00:22:56,698
to a million from about twenty eight

720
00:22:56,698 --> 00:22:57,922
years to about twenty.

721
00:22:58,272 --> 00:23:00,826
Accelerator two: the big three.

722
00:23:01,006 --> 00:23:04,277
Housing, transportation and food make up most

723
00:23:04,277 --> 00:23:05,731
of a typical budget.

724
00:23:05,911 --> 00:23:06,969
A cheaper home,

725
00:23:06,969 --> 00:23:09,861
longer lasting cars and more home cooking

726
00:23:09,861 --> 00:23:12,188
can free up hundreds of dollars a

727
00:23:12,188 --> 00:23:13,528
month, every month,

728
00:23:13,528 --> 00:23:14,375
for decades.

729
00:23:14,725 --> 00:23:17,650
Accelerator three: tax advantages.

730
00:23:17,830 --> 00:23:19,255
Using 401k plans,

731
00:23:19,255 --> 00:23:22,776
IRAs and health savings accounts lets more

732
00:23:22,776 --> 00:23:24,704
of your money compound.

733
00:23:24,884 --> 00:23:26,056
Over thirty years,

734
00:23:26,056 --> 00:23:29,118
the difference between taxed and tax advantaged

735
00:23:29,118 --> 00:23:30,747
growth can be very large.

736
00:23:31,097 --> 00:23:32,901
And accelerator four:

737
00:23:32,901 --> 00:23:33,674
low fees.

738
00:23:33,854 --> 00:23:36,349
A one percent difference in yearly fees

739
00:23:36,349 --> 00:23:38,780
can cost tens of thousands of dollars,

740
00:23:38,780 --> 00:23:41,275
sometimes more than a hundred thousand,

741
00:23:41,275 --> 00:23:43,003
over a long investing life.

742
00:23:43,183 --> 00:23:45,397
Low cost index funds keep more of

743
00:23:45,397 --> 00:23:47,142
the growth in your pocket.

744
00:23:47,492 --> 00:23:49,936
Put all four accelerators together,

745
00:23:49,936 --> 00:23:51,822
and the effect is dramatic.

746
00:23:52,002 --> 00:23:54,775
A household that raises its income over

747
00:23:54,775 --> 00:23:57,120
time, keeps the big three modest,

748
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:00,248
uses every tax advantaged account available,

749
00:24:00,248 --> 00:24:02,594
and pays low fees can often reach

750
00:24:02,594 --> 00:24:05,295
a million years earlier than one doing

751
00:24:05,295 --> 00:24:07,072
only one of those things.

752
00:24:07,252 --> 00:24:08,796
You don't need all four at once.

753
00:24:08,976 --> 00:24:10,021
Add one at a time,

754
00:24:10,021 --> 00:24:11,124
and let them stack.

755
00:24:11,474 --> 00:24:13,384
Let's spend a moment on the long

756
00:24:13,384 --> 00:24:15,831
middle, because it's where the journey is

757
00:24:15,831 --> 00:24:16,547
won or lost.

758
00:24:16,727 --> 00:24:19,591
Between one hundred and five hundred thousand

759
00:24:19,591 --> 00:24:21,500
dollars, the progress is real,

760
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:23,345
but it rarely feels exciting.

761
00:24:23,525 --> 00:24:24,802
Life is busy.

762
00:24:24,982 --> 00:24:27,083
The finish line is far away.

763
00:24:27,263 --> 00:24:28,761
And it's tempting to coast.

764
00:24:29,111 --> 00:24:30,132
Three habits help.

765
00:24:30,312 --> 00:24:33,026
First, raise your contribution every year,

766
00:24:33,026 --> 00:24:34,318
even by one percent.

767
00:24:34,498 --> 00:24:36,618
Second, track your net worth once a

768
00:24:36,618 --> 00:24:38,011
year and write it down,

769
00:24:38,011 --> 00:24:40,010
so you can see the progress you'd

770
00:24:40,010 --> 00:24:40,918
otherwise miss.

771
00:24:41,098 --> 00:24:44,078
Third, find people on the same path,

772
00:24:44,078 --> 00:24:45,733
a friend, a partner,

773
00:24:45,733 --> 00:24:47,637
or an online community,

774
00:24:47,637 --> 00:24:49,457
to keep you motivated.

775
00:24:49,808 --> 00:24:52,048
And give yourself rewards along the way.

776
00:24:52,228 --> 00:24:54,192
A trip when you hit two hundred

777
00:24:54,192 --> 00:24:55,143
fifty thousand.

778
00:24:55,323 --> 00:24:57,749
A nice dinner at three hundred thousand.

779
00:24:57,929 --> 00:24:59,647
The journey should include joy.

780
00:24:59,827 --> 00:25:01,371
That's what makes it sustainable.

781
00:25:01,722 --> 00:25:03,837
Where you hold your investments matters as

782
00:25:03,837 --> 00:25:04,996
you approach a million.

783
00:25:05,176 --> 00:25:09,890
Traditional 401k and IRA withdrawals are generally

784
00:25:09,890 --> 00:25:11,399
taxed as income.

785
00:25:11,579 --> 00:25:13,848
Roth withdrawals can be tax free if

786
00:25:13,848 --> 00:25:15,015
the rules are met.

787
00:25:15,195 --> 00:25:18,678
Taxable brokerage accounts are taxed on dividends

788
00:25:18,678 --> 00:25:21,166
and gains, often at lower long term

789
00:25:21,166 --> 00:25:21,592
rates.

790
00:25:21,943 --> 00:25:24,043
Many people build a mix of all

791
00:25:24,043 --> 00:25:26,633
three, which gives flexibility later.

792
00:25:26,813 --> 00:25:28,173
In low income years,

793
00:25:28,173 --> 00:25:30,892
like early retirement or a career break,

794
00:25:30,892 --> 00:25:33,952
some people convert traditional money to Roth

795
00:25:33,952 --> 00:25:35,312
at a lower tax rate.

796
00:25:35,492 --> 00:25:37,962
A tax professional can help you plan

797
00:25:37,962 --> 00:25:40,844
this, especially in stages eight and nine.

798
00:25:41,194 --> 00:25:43,537
For couples, the journey works best as

799
00:25:43,537 --> 00:25:43,969
a team.

800
00:25:44,149 --> 00:25:45,093
Share the goal,

801
00:25:45,093 --> 00:25:46,731
agree on the savings rate,

802
00:25:46,731 --> 00:25:49,187
and hold a short monthly money meeting.

803
00:25:49,367 --> 00:25:51,765
When both people understand the plan,

804
00:25:51,765 --> 00:25:53,903
it's much easier to stay on track

805
00:25:53,903 --> 00:25:55,393
during expensive years.

806
00:25:55,743 --> 00:25:57,153
For families with kids,

807
00:25:57,153 --> 00:26:00,158
the timeline often stretches during the childcare

808
00:26:00,158 --> 00:26:00,526
years.

809
00:26:00,706 --> 00:26:01,588
That's normal.

810
00:26:01,768 --> 00:26:03,855
Keep at least the match going,

811
00:26:03,855 --> 00:26:05,733
protect the emergency fund,

812
00:26:05,733 --> 00:26:09,211
and raise contributions again when childcare costs

813
00:26:09,211 --> 00:26:09,559
fall.

814
00:26:09,739 --> 00:26:12,186
And put retirement before college savings,

815
00:26:12,186 --> 00:26:14,283
since kids can borrow for school and

816
00:26:14,283 --> 00:26:16,147
you can't borrow for retirement.

817
00:26:16,498 --> 00:26:19,233
And involve your kids in age appropriate

818
00:26:19,233 --> 00:26:19,574
ways.

819
00:26:19,754 --> 00:26:21,113
Show them the plan.

820
00:26:21,293 --> 00:26:23,266
Let them see you save and invest.

821
00:26:23,446 --> 00:26:25,382
Teaching them the playbook early may be

822
00:26:25,382 --> 00:26:27,417
the greatest financial gift you can give.

823
00:26:27,767 --> 00:26:31,442
Mistake one: cashing out retirement accounts when

824
00:26:31,442 --> 00:26:32,492
changing jobs.

825
00:26:32,672 --> 00:26:35,583
Taxes, penalties and lost compounding can cost

826
00:26:35,583 --> 00:26:37,862
far more than the check you receive.

827
00:26:38,042 --> 00:26:39,493
Roll it over instead.

828
00:26:39,843 --> 00:26:42,908
Mistake two: panic selling in a crash.

829
00:26:43,088 --> 00:26:45,446
Selling after a big drop locks in

830
00:26:45,446 --> 00:26:48,591
losses and often means missing the recovery.

831
00:26:48,941 --> 00:26:52,284
Mistake three: chasing get rich quick schemes.

832
00:26:52,464 --> 00:26:54,825
Hot tips, leveraged bets and too good

833
00:26:54,825 --> 00:26:57,249
to be true promises have derailed more

834
00:26:57,249 --> 00:26:58,908
journeys than bad markets.

835
00:26:59,258 --> 00:27:01,487
Mistake four: lifestyle creep.

836
00:27:01,667 --> 00:27:04,288
Letting spending rise as fast as income

837
00:27:04,288 --> 00:27:06,641
keeps the savings rate flat and the

838
00:27:06,641 --> 00:27:08,052
finish line far away.

839
00:27:08,403 --> 00:27:09,618
And mistake five:

840
00:27:09,618 --> 00:27:10,690
never starting.

841
00:27:10,870 --> 00:27:12,706
Waiting for the perfect time,

842
00:27:12,706 --> 00:27:15,491
the perfect income or the perfect knowledge.

843
00:27:15,671 --> 00:27:17,477
The best time to start was years

844
00:27:17,477 --> 00:27:17,702
ago.

845
00:27:17,882 --> 00:27:19,961
The second best time is this week.

846
00:27:20,311 --> 00:27:21,739
Here's your first week.

847
